---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';

/**
 * Same honesty rule as the gig-drivers landing page: state plainly that
 * CabSnap does not track mileage, early. Do not imply the purchases
 * discussed here are deductible.
 */
---

<Article
  slug="receipts-for-doordash-uber-drivers"
  toolLine="Curious what the unrecorded purchases add up to? Four questions, nothing stored."
>
  <p class="lede">
    Nearly every piece of advice written for gig drivers is about mileage. Which
    is reasonable, because for a lot of drivers mileage is the big one &mdash;
    and also means the other half of the paperwork gets almost no attention.
  </p>

  <p>
    This post is about the other half: the things you buy.
  </p>

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>Said plainly up front:</strong> CabSnap does not track mileage.
      Not a lightweight version, not a manual version. If mileage is what you
      need, use a dedicated mileage app &mdash; plenty of drivers run one
      alongside something for receipts, because they genuinely are two different
      problems.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Miles and purchases are different records</h2>

  <p>
    A mileage record is generated by movement. Something observes that you drove
    from here to there and produces a log. Nothing physical changes hands.
  </p>

  <p>
    A purchase record is generated by a transaction, and it produces an object
    &mdash; a slip of paper, an email, a line in an app. That object then has to
    survive until someone needs it, which is where things go wrong.
  </p>

  <p>
    An app that does the first job well has no bearing on the second. A driver
    with immaculate mileage logs and a footwell full of faded gas receipts has
    solved one problem thoroughly and the other not at all.
  </p>

  <h2>The purchases that leave paper</h2>

  <h3>Gas</h3>
  <p>
    Frequent, thermal, and handled outdoors in a hurry &mdash; the same profile
    that makes fuel receipts the most-lost record in trucking. If mileage is
    handled separately, there is a real question for your preparer about how
    fuel is treated alongside it. That is genuinely not a question to answer
    from a blog post, and it is a reason to keep the receipt rather than assume
    it does not matter.
  </p>

  <h3>Car washes</h3>
  <p>
    Small, frequent, and almost never kept. A slip from an automatic wash is
    thermal, goes in the door pocket, and is gone.
  </p>

  <h3>Phone mounts, cables, and chargers</h3>
  <p>
    The quiet consumables of gig work. A mount lasts a few months of constant
    handling. Cables fail. Each purchase is individually forgettable and they
    repeat all year.
  </p>

  <h3>Hot bags, coolers, and insulation</h3>
  <p>
    Bought out of pocket, replaced more often than anyone expects, and rarely
    thought of as business equipment at the moment of purchase &mdash; which is
    exactly why the receipt gets binned with the packaging.
  </p>

  <h3>Tolls and parking</h3>
  <p>
    Individually tiny. Some produce an account statement, which is durable; some
    are cash and produce a slip or nothing. In a dense city these can repeat
    many times a day.
  </p>

  <h3>Cleaning supplies</h3>
  <p>
    Wipes, sprays, air fresheners, the vacuum at the gas station. Rideshare in
    particular generates a steady trickle of this, and none of it feels like a
    business purchase at the time.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this category loses more than trucking does</h2>

  <p>
    An owner-operator generally knows they are running a business. The truck is
    a business asset, the fuel bill is enormous, and the whole thing feels like
    an enterprise with paperwork attached.
  </p>

  <p>
    Gig work does not feel like that. You are driving your own car, the money
    arrives by app, and the purchases are small and domestic &mdash; a car wash,
    a phone cable, a bag. The psychological framing is "buying stuff," not
    "business expenses," and receipts get treated accordingly.
  </p>

  <p>
    The amounts are real regardless of how the purchase felt at the time.
  </p>

  <h2>The card statement trap</h2>

  <p>
    A lot of gig drivers reason that everything is on a card, so the statement
    covers it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Partly true and worth knowing the limits of. Publication 583 does list
    account statements and credit card sales slips among supporting documents,
    so a statement is a legitimate record. But it shows an amount paid to a
    merchant &mdash; not what was purchased.
  </p>

  <p>
    A single stop where you bought gas, a coffee, and a phone cable is one line
    on a statement. Nothing in that line separates the three. Only the itemised
    receipt does that.
  </p>

  <p>
    So the statement is a genuine fallback and a poor substitute. Keeping both
    is not duplication; they prove different things.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do</h2>

  <p>
    Run a mileage app for the miles. For the purchases, capture the receipt in
    the parking lot before the next ping &mdash; while it is in your hand and
    still readable, rather than at the end of a shift when there are eleven of
    them and half have been sat on.
  </p>

  <p>
    And get everything into one place, including the things that arrive as
    email or in an app. Fragmentation is the second-biggest loss after simply
    not capturing.
  </p>

  <p>
    For why the gas slips go blank, see
    <a href="/blog/why-thermal-receipts-fade">why thermal receipts fade</a>. For
    the contractor-side view of the same problem, see
    <a href="/guide/1099-tax-deductions">1099 contractor expense records</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
